│ patch_name               │ max_min_ratio_anneal_14pts                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The original program just placed 14 random points, giving a terrible min/max distance     │
│                          │ ratio. Instead, I use a two-phase approach inspired by known spherical codes: (1)         │
│                          │ initialize with a well-spread configuration on the unit sphere (icosphere-like plus       │
│                          │ optimized placement), then (2) run a repulsion-based optimization where all pairwise      │
│                          │ distances are pushed toward the maximum distance using a soft-min "log-sum-exp" penalty   │
│                          │ on small distances. This effectively performs simulated-annealing-style gradient descent  │
│                          │ on the min/max ratio objective directly. Points are normalized to the unit sphere each    │
│                          │ step (scaling-invariant objective makes this a safe constraint), with the final           │
│                          │ configuration rescaled so the maximum distance equals a fixed value. This is a            │
│                          │ fundamentally different approach from random placement: it directly optimizes the target  │
│                          │ metric with gradient-based continuous optimization and multiple random restarts with      │
│                          │ fixed seeds for reproducibility.                                                          │
