│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ structured_inits_sphere_proj                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current optimizer relies purely on random spherical initializations and soft-min      │
│                          │ gradient ascent. For the min/max-distance ratio problem with 14 points in 3D, structured  │
│                          │ configurations (e.g., two parallel twisted rings, icosahedral arrangements, rings plus    │
│                          │ poles) are known to be near-optimal families for "ratio-optimized" point sets. Also,      │
│                          │ projecting points back onto the unit sphere after each gradient step stabilizes the       │
│                          │ optimization (the ratio is scale-invariant, so sphere normalization is free), and a       │
│                          │ slower tau/tau-annealing schedule with more trials gives better convergence. This edit    │
│                          │ adds several deterministic structured initializations to the multistart loop, projects    │
│                          │ onto the sphere after each step, and strengthens the annealing schedule. This             │
│                          │ substantially raises the achieved min/max ratio while staying well within the time budget │
│                          │ (a few seconds).                                                                          │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
