                        Patch Metadata - Gen 14/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ softmin_annealing_with_antiprism_seeds                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current SLSQP formulation uses a hard min-max constraint (t² ≤ d² for all pairs),     │
│                          │ which is highly non-smooth and frequently stalls at poor local optima (score 0.07).       │
│                          │ Replace it with a smooth annealed objective: minimize `τ·logsumexp(-d²/τ) +               │
│                          │ τ·logsumexp(d²/τ)` over the raw coordinates with L-BFGS-B. The first term is a smooth     │
│                          │ approximation of `-min(d²)` (push smallest distances up) and the second a smooth          │
│                          │ approximation of `max(d²)` (acts as a soft scale normalization, keeping the diameter in   │
│                          │ check). Annealing τ from coarse to fine gradually sharpens both terms toward the true     │
│                          │ min/max objective, which is far more robust than the cold-started hard constraint solve.  │
│                          │ Keep the original SLSQP as a cheap final polish on the annealed point.                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Additionally, add structured seeds known to be near-optimal geometries for 14 points: the │
│                          │ heptagonal antiprism (two staggered 7-rings at several heights/radii — this family is a   │
│                          │ strong candidate for the 14-point optimum, analogous to how anti-prisms are               │
│                          │ optimal/competitive in related Tammes-type problems), plus a hexagonal-close-packed-like  │
│                          │ layered seed. Annealing from these structured starts plus the existing icosahedron+poles  │
│                          │ seeds gives much better chances of reaching the reference squared ratio ≈ 0.24.           │
