│ patch_description        │ The current best score (0.99) corresponds to a squared ratio of ~0.2397, essentially at   │
│                          │ the reference 0.24 but not above it. The annealing ladder stops at T=0.002, so the        │
│                          │ smoothed min/max ratio still slightly differs from the exact ratio dmin/dmax; the         │
│                          │ optimizer can terminate before the exact binding constraints (closest and farthest pairs) │
│                          │ are fully balanced. The most direct way to squeeze out the remaining gap is a final       │
│                          │ low-temperature polish stage run only on the best configuration found so far: continue    │
│                          │ the annealing ladder down to T=0.0008 and 0.0003 with tighter L-BFGS-B tolerances, and    │
│                          │ wrap it in a few small-magnitude perturbation restarts (magnitude ~0.004-0.01, far        │
│                          │ smaller than the 0.02+ used in the existing restarts, so we stay in the basin of the good │
│                          │ optimum while escaping tiny numerical plateaus). Each candidate is scored with the exact  │
│                          │ `ratio()`, and the best is kept. This is cheap (only a handful of extra L-BFGS-B runs on  │
│                          │ 42 variables), preserves the existing structure, and directly targets the metric the      │
│                          │ evaluator computes — the exact dmin/dmax, not the smoothed surrogate.                     │
