                        Patch Metadata - Gen 12/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ smooth_ratio_adam_multistart                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current optimizer uses a hand-rolled force heuristic (pushing all pairs below the     │
│                          │ current max distance apart) with annealing noise, which stalls around ratio ≈ 0.44 (score │
│                          │ 0.81). The reference optimum is ratio ≈ 0.49 (squared ratio 1/4.1658), so there is        │
│                          │ significant headroom.                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key idea: optimize a *smooth, differentiable* surrogate of the actual objective       │
│                          │ dmin/dmax directly, namely J = softmin_τ(d_ij) / softmax_τ(d_ij), with the temperature τ  │
│                          │ annealed from 0.15 down to ~0.01 so the surrogate converges to the true ratio. Since J is │
│                          │ fully differentiable in the point coordinates, we can use Adam (with step clipping and    │
│                          │ centroid/scale normalization to exploit translation and scale invariance) for far more    │
│                          │ stable and precise convergence than the previous discrete force rule. The analytic        │
│                          │ gradient is:                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - dJ/d(d_ij) = (w_ij - J·u_ij) / B, where w are softmin weights (dA/dd) and u are softmax │
│                          │ weights (dB/dd).                                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This automatically pushes near-minimal pairs apart AND pulls near-maximal pairs together, │
│                          │ which the old scheme only did crudely (and it never pulled the diameter pairs in).        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Additional improvements:                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. Multi-start over 10 random inits plus one structured init (two staggered regular       │
│                          │ heptagons, a plausible basin for the 14-point diameter-packing optimum).                  │
│                          │ 2. Best-configuration tracking at every iteration (since the ratio is non-smooth, the     │
│                          │ true objective is evaluated each step and the best snapshot kept).                        │
│                          │ 3. A deterministic sharpening polish phase (small τ, small learning rate) on the best     │
│                          │ candidate across restarts.                                                                │
│                          │ 4. Numerically stabilized softmin/softmax (shift exponent arguments by dmin/dmax to avoid │
│                          │ overflow at small τ).                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Iter count (~25k total) is similar to the old program (~24k), so eval time stays ~4s,     │
│                          │ well within the timeout.                                                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0412                                                                                   │
