│ patch_name               │ add_pair_move_hillclimb                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The annealed soft-min/soft-max L-BFGS approach plateaus near 0.24 because gradient steps  │
│                          │ treat all pairwise distances nearly uniformly, while the true objective depends only on   │
│                          │ the min-distance pair and max-distance pair. Add a fast, targeted hill-climb that (a)     │
│                          │ pushes the closest pair of points directly apart along their separating direction, and    │
│                          │ (b) pulls the farthest pair inward along theirs, with adaptive step sizes and a shrinking │
│                          │ geometric schedule. These moves act exactly on the binding constraints of dmin/dmax, so   │
│                          │ they can squeeze out extra ratio that coordinate-based optimizers miss. Insert it as      │
│                          │ Phase 4 before normalization, and trim Phase 2/3 trial counts slightly to offset added    │
│                          │ runtime (current eval_time 51.88s is high; keep under timeout). The hill-climb is cheap   │
│                          │ (O(n^2) per iteration, no gradients), and since every accepted move must strictly         │
│                          │ increase the true ratio, it can only improve the returned configuration.                  │
