│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_pair_hillclimb_reanneal                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current pair-move hill-climb only moves the single closest and single farthest pair.  │
│                          │ Two upgrades inspired by the binding-constraint structure of the optimal Tammes-like      │
│                          │ configurations: (1) consider several near-minimal and near-maximal pairs (often the       │
│                          │ optimum has multiple simultaneously-binding constraints — e.g. several edges tied at dmin │
│                          │ in the D6/ring family), moving each along its connecting direction; (2) after the         │
│                          │ hill-climb, re-anneal the result at low temperature with L-BFGS-B (the hill-climb point   │
│                          │ is generally not a local optimum of the smooth objective, and re-annealing lets all 14    │
│                          │ coordinates relax), then hill-climb again — alternate for a few rounds. This closes the   │
│                          │ gap the coordinate moves cannot reach while keeping runtime bounded. I also slightly      │
│                          │ reduce Phase 2/3 work to compensate for the added time.                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 5                                                                                         │
