╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_seed_and_tangential_pair_hillclimb                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current 0.99 score is close to the reference squared ratio (1/4.1658 ≈ 0.24004). Two  │
│                          │ targeted improvements: (1) add structured seed configurations — the D6h family (two       │
│                          │ staggered hexagonal rings plus two axial poles), which is the suspected optimal symmetry  │
│                          │ class for n=14 in 3D — to the multi-start annealed soft-min polish, so the optimizer      │
│                          │ starts in the best basin rather than only the icosahedron+2 basin. (2) After the          │
│                          │ axis-aligned greedy polish, add a targeted pair-separation hill-climb that moves the      │
│                          │ closest pair apart along the connecting direction plus tangential variants (letting the   │
│                          │ pair slide around third-neighbor obstacles, as pure axial moves get blocked), and         │
│                          │ simultaneously pulls the farthest pair inward, with step halving. This attacks the exact  │
│                          │ min/max pair bottleneck directly and can push the squared ratio to/past the reference     │
│                          │ 0.24004. Both changes are bounded in iterations to stay within the timeout.               │
