│ patch_description        │ All three programs are stuck at ratio 0.22 (score 0.93–0.94), suggesting the              │
│                          │ deterministic axial pair moves in `climb` and the soft-min/soft-max polish converge to a  │
│                          │ local optimum whose binding constraints (near-min and near-max pairs) cannot be           │
│                          │ rearranged by the existing moves. Following the recommendation, I add a guarded           │
│                          │ basin-probe stage that runs several trials perturbing only the points involved in         │
│                          │ near-binding min/max pairs (±0.01–0.02 along random unit directions), each followed by    │
│                          │ the existing polish + hard climb, keeping the best exact ratio. Because only a handful of │
│                          │ points are moved, the configuration stays in the same basin while exploring directions    │
│                          │ orthogonal to the axial moves; the strict "keep only improvements" guard ensures the      │
│                          │ incumbent can never degrade. I also reduce the expensive tight-restart loop from 8 to 5   │
│                          │ iterations to offset the added runtime and stay well within the timeout.                  │
